-- title:
--   Bounded denominators for rational weight-2m modular functions
-- statement:
--   Fix $N \geq 1$, a natural number $m$, and a Laurent series $X \in \mathbb{Q}(\!(q)\!)$. Two hypotheses are imposed. First, $X$ lies in [`ModularCurve.modularFunctionFieldFull N`](def/ModularCurve_X0.html#L305), the intermediate field of $\mathbb{Q}(\!(q)\!)$ over $\mathbb{Q}$ generated by the set of series $\mathrm{qExpand}_{\mathbb{Q}}(d, \mathrm{jq})$ for the nonzero divisors $d$ of $N$, i.e. by the $q$-expansions of $j$ with $q$ replaced by $q^{d}$. Second, [`ModularCurve.IsModPFormFn ℚ m X`](def/ModularCurve_ModPFormFn.html#L20) holds: writing $j =$ `jqModC ℚ` $= q^{-1}\cdot(\text{image of the integral power series } \mathrm{jNum})$, the element $X^{6} j^{4m} (j - 1728)^{3m}$ is integral over the $\mathbb{Q}$-subalgebra $\mathbb{Q}[j]$ of $\mathbb{Q}(\!(q)\!)$, and $X^{2} j^{m} (j - 1728)^{m}$ is integral over the $\mathbb{Q}$-subalgebra $\mathbb{Q}[j^{-1}]$. The conclusion asserts the existence of a nonzero integer $c$ and a Laurent series $Y$ with integer coefficients such that the coefficientwise image of $Y$ under $\mathbb{Z} \to \mathbb{Q}$ equals $c \cdot X$; that is, $cX$ has integral $q$-expansion.
--
--   This is the bounded-denominator, or integrality, step: a modular function of level $N$ with rational $q$-expansion satisfying the two holomorphy-type integrality conditions of weight $2m$ becomes integral after scaling by a single nonzero rational integer. It is used in [`ModularForm.exists_linearIndependent_int_qCoeff_dimFormula_le_card`](thm.html#ModularForm.exists_linearIndependent_int_qCoeff_dimFormula_le_card) to pass from bases of rational modular functions to families with integral $q$-expansions.
